MOD 1 - PRELIMINARY EVALUATION OF n + 241U REACTIONS              9246 1451   44
                                                                  9246 1451   45
                   P. G. Young and M. B. Chadwick                 9246 1451   46
                                                                  9246 1451   47
                      Theoretical Division                        9246 1451   48
                Los Alamos National Laboratory                    9246 1451   49
                      Los Alamos, NM 87545                        9246 1451   50
                       September, 2000                            9246 1451   51
                                                                  9246 1451   52
I. SUMMARY                                                        9246 1451   53
                                                                  9246 1451   54
   The cross section data file for neutron reactions with 241U    9246 1451   55
has been evaluated over the incident neutron energy range from    9246 1451   56
10-5 eV to 30 MeV.  This work utilizes results from a new         9246 1451   57
theoretical study, which includes a systematic analysis of        9246 1451   58
first-chance fission data from 232,233,234,235,236,238U + n       9246 1451   59
reactions.  There are no experimental data for 241U.              9246 1451   60
                                                                  9246 1451   61
   Details of the present analysis are given in the sections      9246 1451   62
that follow.  Some general features of the work are:              9246 1451   63
                                                                  9246 1451   64
1.  The ENDF/B-VI n + 237U resonance parameters were adopted for  9246 1451   65
n + 241U at neutron energies below 10 keV, as modified in the     9246 1451   66
Los Alamos 1998 evaluation of n + 237U reactions (Yo98).  The     9246 1451   67
resonance analysis was suitably joined with the present analysis  9246 1451   68
at 10 keV.                                                        9246 1451   69
                                                                  9246 1451   70
2.  A systematic analysis was performed of experimental fission   9246 1451   71
and radiative capture cross section data for uranium isotopes     9246 1451   72
between 232U and 238U.  An isospin-dependent coupled-channel      9246 1451   73
optical model potential was developed for the uranium isotopes    9246 1451   74
and was used to calculate the n + 241U neutron total cross        9246 1451   75
section, elastic and inelastic scattering cross sections,         9246 1451   76
elastic scattering angular distributions, and reaction cross      9246 1451   77
sections.  Transmission coefficients were obtained from the       9246 1451   78
optical model for theoretical calculations with reaction theory   9246 1451   79
codes.                                                            9246 1451   80
                                                                  9246 1451   81
3.  Photon production data and fission neutron spectra were       9246 1451   82
taken from the existing ENDF/B-VI evaluation for n + 237U         9246 1451   83
reactions. Prompt ubar was estimated from systematics.            9246 1451   84
                                                                  9246 1451   85
                                                                  9246 1451   86
II.  THEORETICAL ANALYSIS                                         9246 1451   87
                                                                  9246 1451   88
  The basic approach followed was to fit the available            9246 1451   89
experimental data for (n,f), (n,g), (n,xn), and other reactions   9246 1451   90
for the stable uranium isotopes using nuclear models, and to      9246 1451   91
then use reasonable extrapolations of the parameters to infer     9246 1451   92
cross sections for 241U.  To perform the analysis, we utilized    9246 1451   93
the Hauser-Feshbach reaction theory code COMNUC, the Hauser-      9246 1451   94
Feshbach/preequilibrium reaction theory code GNASH, and the       9246 1451   95
coupled-channels optical model code ECIS.                         9246 1451   96
                                                                  9246 1451   97
    A.  Optical Model                                             9246 1451   98
                                                                  9246 1451   99
   The deformed optical model potential was obtained by           9246 1451  100
considering several coupled-channel parameterizations developed   9246 1451  101
from n + 235,238U and 239Pu experimental data , several of which  9246 1451  102
were used in ENDF/B-VI evaluations.  The Harwell potential by     9246 1451  103
Madland and Young (Ma78), the Bruyeres-le-Chatel (BRC) potential  9246 1451  104
by Lagrange (Ha82), and a modified version of the BRC potential   9246 1451  105
by Arthur and Young (Yo88, Yo92a) were examined.  Isospin         9246 1451  106
corrections were applied to all the potentials to adapt them for  9246 1451  107
the various U isotopes.  Corrections from the measured to the     9246 1451  108
unmeasured cases were made in the deformation parameters b2 and   9246 1451  109
b4, using a simple model by D. Madland to calculate deformation   9246 1451  110
parameters for targets without experimental information from      9246 1451  111
deformations given in the extensive Nix-Moller mass analysis.     9246 1451  112
For the optical model potential, we chose to use an isospin       9246 1451  113
version of the BRC potential (Ha82), which was inferred from      9246 1451  114
Lagrange's results for individual nuclides.  The lowest three     9246 1451  115
members of the 7/2+ ground state rotational band were included    9246 1451  116
in the calculations, that is, the 9/2+ and 11/2+ states at Ex =   9246 1451  117
56 and 123 keV were coupled with the 7/2+ ground state.  All of   9246 1451  118
the potentials considered gave total and reaction cross sections  9246 1451  119
that were reasonably consistent, so the selection was based       9246 1451  120
mainly on the fact that the BRC potential gave better agreement   9246 1451  121
with fission cross sections for odd U isotopes (233,235U) at      9246 1451  122
lower energies.                                                   9246 1451  123
                                                                  9246 1451  124
   B.  Reaction Theory Calculations                               9246 1451  125
                                                                  9246 1451  126
   The Hauser-Feshbach statistical calculations were performed    9246 1451  127
with the COMNUC (Du70) and GNASH (Yo92b) codes.  Both codes       9246 1451  128
include a double-humped fission barrier model, using uncoupled    9246 1451  129
oscillators for the barrier representation in GNASH and coupled   9246 1451  130
or uncoupled oscillators in COMNUC, as described by Arthur        9246 1451  131
(Ar82a).  The COMNUC calculations include the possibility of      9246 1451  132
width-fluctuation corrections and corrections for class II        9246 1451  133
fluctuations, which are needed at lower energies, whereas GNASH   9246 1451  134
provides the preequilibrium corrections that are required at      9246 1451  135
higher energies.  Accordingly, COMNUC was used in the             9246 1451  136
calculations below about 0.5 MeV, utilizing a non-resonant model  9246 1451  137
and including class II fluctuations, and the GNASH code was       9246 1451  138
employed at higher energies using two uncoupled oscillators in    9246 1451  139
the fission calculations.                                         9246 1451  140
   Each compound nucleus formed in the calculations is permitted  9246 1451  141
to decay through the fission channel, by neutron emission, and    9246 1451  142
by gamma-ray emission.  Neutron transmission coefficients for     9246 1451  143
the Hauser-Feshbach calculations are obtained from the coupled-   9246 1451  144
channel optical model.  Gamma ray transmission coefficients are   9246 1451  145
obtained from gamma-ray strength functions calculated with the    9246 1451  146
generalized Lorentzian model of Kopecky and Uhl (Ko90).           9246 1451  147
Transmission coefficients for fission are calculated from the     9246 1451  148
fission model summarized here and detailed in Yo92b.              9246 1451  149
   Gilbert and Cameron (Gi70) phenomenological level density      9246 1451  150
functions were used to represent continuum levels at ground-      9246 1451  151
state deformations, appropriately matched to available            9246 1451  152
experimental structure data at lower excitation energies.         9246 1451  153
Multiplicative factors were applied to the level density          9246 1451  154
functions to account for enhancements in the fission transition   9246 1451  155
state densities at the fission barriers due to increased          9246 1451  156
asymmetry conditions, and the continuum level densities were      9246 1451  157
matched to the discrete fission transition states at each         9246 1451  158
barrier.   The discrete fission transition state spectra were     9246 1451  159
calculated from bandhead information taken from calculations and  9246 1451  160
compilations by Britt (Br82).                                     9246 1451  161
   Initial values of fission barrier parameters were taken from   9246 1451  162
the work of Britt (Br82,Ba74), and we used default level density  9246 1451  163
parameters of Gilbert and Cameron for all ground state nuclei.    9246 1451  164
We only modified the Britt-Back parameters in very simple ways    9246 1451  165
in order to facilitate extrapolation of the parameters.  Our      9246 1451  166
modifications were limited to minor adjustments of the fission    9246 1451  167
barrier heights, the pairing energy at the fission barriers       9246 1451  168
(although we usually used the Gilbert-Cameron values), and the    9246 1451  169
level matching of the temperature region of the continuum level   9246 1451  170
densities at the fission saddles.                                 9246 1451  171
   We fit the experimental (n,f) and (n,g) cross sections for     9246 1451  172
all stable isotopes over the energy region of first-chance        9246 1451  173
fission using the COMNUC model code.  Experimental data exists    9246 1451  174
for targets of 232U, 233U, 234U, 235U, 236U, and 238U, which      9246 1451  175
allowed us to infer fission barrier parameters and gamma-ray      9246 1451  176
strength functions for the compound systems of 233U, 234U, 235U,  9246 1451  177
236U, 237U, and 239U.  Using the parameters determined from the   9246 1451  178
first-chance fission fitting without modification, we performed   9246 1451  179
GNASH calculations over the entire energy region of each target   9246 1451  180
nucleus.  We carried the calculations to 30 MeV, which involve    9246 1451  181
first-, second-, third-, fourth-, and fifth-chance fission        9246 1451  182
processes.   These comparisons provide a necessary test of the    9246 1451  183
reliability of our calculations, in that no adjustments are made  9246 1451  184
past the first-chance fission results.                            9246 1451  185
                                                                  9246 1451  186
III.  DESCRIPTION OF EVALUATED DATA BASE                          9246 1451  187
                                                                  9246 1451  188
   A.  Total Cross Section                                        9246 1451  189
                                                                  9246 1451  190
   The evaluated total cross section below 10 keV is obtained     9246 1451  191
from the n + 237U resonance parameter evaluation in ENDF/B-VI,    9246 1451  192
as modified in a recent Los Alamos evaluation (Yo98).  From 10    9246 1451  193
keV to 30 MeV, the coupled-channel deformed optical model         9246 1451  194
calculations described above are used directly.                   9246 1451  195
                                                                  9246 1451  196
   B.  Elastic Cross Section                                      9246 1451  197
                                                                  9246 1451  198
   The elastic cross section at all energies is obtained from     9246 1451  199
the difference of the total and nonelastic cross sections. Below  9246 1451  200
10 keV it comes from the resonance parameters and at higher       9246 1451  201
energies it is determined by the coupled-channel optical model    9246 1451  202
calculations.  Angular distributions for elastic neutrons were    9246 1451  203
obtained at all energies from the ECIS coupled-channel            9246 1451  204
calculations combined with the COMNUC compound-elastic            9246 1451  205
calculations (see above) and are given as Legendre expansions.    9246 1451  206
                                                                  9246 1451  207
   C.  Fission Cross Section                                      9246 1451  208
                                                                  9246 1451  209
   The fission cross section is calculated from the resonance     9246 1451  210
parameters below 10 keV, as described earlier.  At higher         9246 1451  211
energies the fission cross section is based on the theoretical    9246 1451  212
analysis described above.                                         9246 1451  213
                                                                  9246 1451  214
   D.  Radiative Capture Cross Section                            9246 1451  215
                                                                  9246 1451  216
   Below 10 keV, the capture cross section is based on the        9246 1451  217
resonance parameters.  At higher energies the cross section is    9246 1451  218
calculated using the generalized Lorentzian model of Ko90.  The   9246 1451  219
normalization of the strength function was set by requiring the   9246 1451  220
calculated (n,g) cross section be consistent with measured        9246 1451  221
values for 235U, similar to our n + 237U evaluation               9246 1451  222
                                                                  9246 1451  223
   E.  Inelastic Neutron Cross Sections                           9246 1451  224
                                                                  9246 1451  225
   Discrete (n,n') cross sections are included for the lowest 2   9246 1451  226
rotational levels of 241U (MT=51,52)and for 25 (representative)   9246 1451  227
collective levels (MT=53-77).  Because the structure of 241U is   9246 1451  228
unknown, the rotational states were estimated from a rotational   9246 1451  229
model.  The two excited states are members of the K = 7/2 ground- 9246 1451  230
state rotational band; the cross sections and angular             9246 1451  231
distributions include direct components from the ECIS             9246 1451  232
calculations as well as compound nucleus contributions from the   9246 1451  233
COMNUC calculations.  The other collective levels (MT=53-78) are  9246 1451  234
based on calculations for 235U (MT=53-58) and 238U (MT=59-77),    9246 1451  235
with the assumption that similar states exist for 241U.  The      9246 1451  236
angular distributions of all excited levels are represented by    9246 1451  237
Legendre expansions.                                              9246 1451  238
   The (n,n'continuum) inelastic data (resulting from compound    9246 1451  239
nucleus and preequilibrium reactions at excitation energies in    9246 1451  240
241U above 0.123 MeV) are given as energy-angle correlated        9246 1451  241
continuous spectra and were calculated with the GNASH code.  The  9246 1451  242
results are given in File 6 of the ENDF/B-formatted evaluation    9246 1451  243
(ENDF-6 format) and utilize systematics by Kalbach (Ka88) for     9246 1451  244
angular distribution information, parameterized in terms of       9246 1451  245
preequilibrium ratios calculated in GNASH.                        9246 1451  246
                                                                  9246 1451  247
   F.  Cross Sections for 241U(n,xn) Reactions                    9246 1451  248
                                                                  9246 1451  249
   The 241U(n,2n), (n,3n), and (n,4n) cross sections result       9246 1451  250
entirely from the theoretical analysis and GNASH calculations.    9246 1451  251
Energy-angle correlated distributions are given for all the       9246 1451  252
241U(n,xn) reactions, making use of Kalbach's systematics (Ka88)  9246 1451  253
and the ENDF/B-6 File 6 representation.                           9246 1451  254
                                                                  9246 1451  255
   G.  Nubar                                                      9246 1451  256
                                                                  9246 1451  257
   The evaluation of delayed nubar were carried over directly     9246 1451  258
from the ENDF/B-VI evaluation of 237U.  The prompt nubar          9246 1451  259
evaluation is based on the systematics of Manero and Konshin      9246 1451  260
(Ma72), updated using experimental values for targets of 235U     9246 1451  261
and 238U .                                                        9246 1451  262
                                                                  9246 1451  263
   H.  Fission Neutron Spectra                                    9246 1451  264
                                                                  9246 1451  265
   The fission neutron spectra in the revised evaluation is       9246 1451  266
carried over directly from the 237U evaluation in ENDF/B-VI.  A   9246 1451  267
maxwellian representation is used for the spectrum shape, with    9246 1451  268
energy-dependent temperature parameters based on systematics.     9246 1451  269
                                                                  9246 1451  270
                                                                  9246 1451  271
IV.  CONCLUSIONS                                                  9246 1451  272
                                                                  9246 1451  273
   The new analysis of n + 241U nuclear data should provide an    9246 1451  274
approximate representation of most reactions over the incident    9246 1451  275
energy range 10 keV to 30 MeV.  This evaluation is regarded as    9246 1451  276
preliminary until some of our assumptions concerning structure    9246 1451  277
and fission barriers can be better assessed.  Theoretical         9246 1451  278
estimates of the structure of 241U are needed.  Also, further     9246 1451  279
work is needed to obtain better theoretical estimates of all the  9246 1451  280
cross sections but particularly fission.  Additionally,           9246 1451  281
improvements to the evaluations of nubar and the fission neutron  9246 1451  282
spectra using the Madland-Nix approach will be considered in the  9246 1451  283
future.                                                           9246 1451  284
                                                                  9246 1451  285
